   $q$How does the second paragraph build on the first?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It argues that lichens are harmed less by pollution than other plants because they have no roots.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It shows that the trait letting lichens live without soil, taking everything from the air, also makes them sensitive to pollution.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It repeats the description of where lichens grow, adding more examples of surfaces such as rock and bark.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"It introduces a new topic, the history of air pollution laws, that the rest of the passage then explains.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows that the trait letting lichens live without soil, taking everything from the air, also makes them sensitive to pollution.$q$, $q$Paragraph 2 opens with "That same independence," linking the soil-free life described in paragraph 1 to the sensitivity that makes lichens useful. It says the lack of roots and a waxy layer makes lichens more exposed, not less.$q$, 'draft', '11940ca5-259d-5c38-9ece-ea1fc10e8679')
