-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b9a9e3e-ad90-5eb8-8cbb-7f6932e21313', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Under the Yellow Varnish$q$, $q$Visitors to a museum often assume that an old painting looks the way it did the day the artist finished it. In many cases, it does not. Over decades, the protective coat of varnish that painters brushed over their work can yellow and darken, turning a clear blue sky into a muddy green and dulling bright skin tones to brown. What looks like the artist's style may actually be the effect of time.

Removing that old varnish is one of the most delicate jobs a conservator does. Varnish and paint can both dissolve in the same kinds of solvents, so the conservator must find a mixture strong enough to soften the varnish but too weak to disturb the paint below. Working under magnification, often with small cotton swabs, a conservator may spend months cleaning a single canvas a few square inches at a time.

Modern conservators also follow a principle that earlier restorers did not always respect: anything added to a painting should be removable. When a conservator fills a small loss in the paint, the new material is chosen so that a future expert could take it out without harm. New varnish is selected for the same reason and for its resistance to yellowing.

This careful approach grew out of hard lessons. At the fictional Ostrava Art Hall, records show that a restorer in the 1920s cleaned a landscape so aggressively that he removed thin glazes of color the artist had applied on purpose. The painting has looked flat ever since, and nothing can bring those glazes back.

Cleaning also raises a question with no simple answer: how far back should a painting be returned? Some conservators argue that a slight warm tone is part of a work's history and should stay. Others aim for the brightest original colors that can be safely recovered. In practice, most decisions are made one painting at a time, after study and discussion.$q$, 'draft')
