-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0f82031e-88f1-5a7f-a969-d3e6f4fc3e6a', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Give Westmere Back Its Stars$q$, $q$Ask anyone who grew up in the fictional town of Westmere about summer nights, and you will probably hear about the stars. Older residents describe lying in the field behind the school and tracing the pale band of the galaxy across the sky. Today a child in the same field would struggle to find more than a few dozen stars. The sky has not changed. Our lighting has.

Westmere should adopt a dark-sky lighting standard for all new and replaced outdoor lights. This would not mean turning off streetlights or leaving sidewalks dark. It would mean using fixtures that point light down where people need it instead of up and sideways, choosing warmer, dimmer bulbs where bright white ones are unnecessary, and putting timers on lights that shine all night over empty parking lots.

The first reason is simple waste. A light that shines into the sky illuminates nothing anyone uses, yet the town still pays for the electricity. The town's own energy report found that a third of the lighting budget goes to fixtures that scatter much of their light upward. Shielded fixtures would let the town use lower-power bulbs and still light the ground just as well.

The second reason is the effect on living things. Night-flying insects gather around unshielded lights by the thousands, and many never leave. Migrating birds can be pulled off course by brightly lit buildings. Even people sleep less deeply when strong light leaks through bedroom windows.

Some residents worry that less light will mean less safety. That concern deserves a serious answer. Glare from unshielded lights actually makes it harder to see into shadows, while shielded lights spread even light across a sidewalk without blinding drivers or walkers. Safety depends on light reaching the right places, not on the total amount poured into the night.

A lighting standard asks little of anyone and offers a great deal back: lower bills, healthier wildlife and, on clear nights, a sky our grandparents would recognize.$q$, 'draft')
