-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d931dcbc-3463-57a9-9989-863b2dad859c', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Four Seconds$q$, $q$Film night on the library lawn had happened every August for eleven years, and for eleven years Noor Haddad's cousin Sami had sat in the back row, reading the movie from the actors' faces and guessing the rest. Sami had been deaf since birth. This year Noor had promised captions.

Her plan was simple on paper. The projector showed the film on a white sheet stretched between two poles. Noor had written a short program that read the subtitle file and displayed each line on a long LED sign borrowed from the school's theater department, mounted along the bottom of the frame.

At the test run on Thursday, everything worked. At 8:15 on Saturday, with ninety people on blankets and the opening music already playing, the captions appeared four seconds early.

Four seconds does not sound like much. But the caption for the first joke appeared before the actor said it, and the people who could read the sign laughed before the people watching the screen did. Somebody in the front row turned around to look at the sign, then at Noor.

She crouched over her laptop behind the projector table. The file was fine. The program was fine. She guessed that the projector itself was holding the video back for a moment before playing it, something it had not done on Thursday because she had tested with a shorter clip. She could not change the projector. She could only make her own program wait longer, and she did not know exactly how much longer.

She tried three seconds. Then four. Then three and a half, watching the actors' mouths instead of the sign, the way Sami had for eleven years.

At the end of the first half hour, she looked up. Sami had moved. He was sitting on the third blanket from the front, between two kids she did not know, and when the next joke came, he laughed exactly when they did.

Noor did not touch the laptop again for the rest of the night.$q$, 'draft')
