-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('df66d435-30bc-5035-a2b4-bf514dabf824', 'rg11-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Lives in One Crust$q$, $q$On a bare boulder or an old fence post, you may notice crusty patches of gray, green or orange. These are lichens, and although they look like a single plant, each one is actually a partnership between two very different kinds of living things. Understanding that partnership explains how lichens survive in places where almost nothing else can.

The first partner is a fungus. Like a mushroom, it cannot make its own food from sunlight. What it can do is build a tough, spongy body that clings to rock, soaks up rainwater and shields whatever lives inside it from drying winds and harsh light. The second partner is an alga or a kind of bacterium that, like a plant, captures sunlight and turns it into sugar. On its own, this partner would quickly dry out on an exposed rock. Inside the fungus, it is sheltered.

Each partner, in other words, supplies what the other lacks. The fungus provides a house with plumbing, and the light-capturing partner pays the rent in sugar. Neither could settle on a bare stone alone, but together they can live on surfaces that receive little more than sunshine, rain and dust.

This arrangement makes lichens important pioneers. Over many years, they release weak acids that slowly break down the rock beneath them, and their dead fragments mix with dust to form the first thin layer of soil. Mosses and small plants can then take root where lichens prepared the ground.

Lichens also serve as living alarm systems. Because they absorb nearly everything that falls on them from the air and rain, many kinds are sensitive to pollution and disappear where the air is dirty. Scientists sometimes survey which lichens grow on trees in a region as an inexpensive way to judge its air quality.

Lichens are easy to overlook, but their quiet cooperation shows that some of the toughest survivors in nature are not individuals at all.$q$, 'draft')
