-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dd6e4043-1c3e-514b-aa63-9a0ad0b3b00d', 'rg11-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why a Spinning Ball Bends$q$, $q$A soccer player lines up a free kick twenty meters from the goal. A wall of defenders stands between her and the net, blocking the straight path. She strikes the ball off center, and it sails to the right of the wall, then swerves left in midair and dips under the crossbar. To the goalkeeper, it can look as if the ball changed its mind. In fact, it was obeying a principle that physicists understand well.

The key is spin. When the player strikes the ball off center, she sets it rotating as well as moving forward. As the ball travels, its surface drags a thin layer of air around with it. On one side, the surface turns in the same direction as the air streaming past, so the air clings to the ball and follows its curve toward the back. On the other side, the surface turns against the passing air, which slows the flow and makes it break away from the ball sooner.

The result is lopsided. Because the air leaving the back of the ball is thrown off toward one side, the ball is pushed toward the other, much as a rowboat moves forward when its oars push water backward. Scientists call this sideways push the Magnus effect, after a researcher who studied it in the nineteenth century.

You can see the effect without a soccer ball. Tape two paper cups together at their bottoms, loop a rubber band around the middle and launch the cups by letting the band unwind beneath them, so that they spin backward as they fly forward. Instead of dropping in a simple arc, the cups rise or even loop through the air.

The same principle appears across many sports. A tennis player uses topspin to make the ball dip sharply into the court, a pitcher throws a curveball that breaks away from the batter, and a golfer relies on backspin to keep the ball aloft longer. Once you understand spin, many of the most surprising moments in sports become predictable.$q$, 'draft')
