-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d71f164e-7216-5796-85a0-91b9105c189d', 'rg11-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bringing a Painting Back to Light$q$, $q$When the Harrowgate Museum received a landscape painting found in a long-forgotten attic, the canvas was so dark that visitors could barely make out a river and a line of hills. The museum conservator, Amara Okafor, spent eleven months restoring it. Her work followed a careful sequence, and each step depended on the one before it.

She began not with a brush but with a camera and a microscope. Okafor photographed the painting under ordinary light, ultraviolet light and infrared light. Each kind of light revealed something different: ultraviolet showed where earlier repairers had added paint, and infrared revealed the artist's original pencil sketch beneath the surface. These images became a map she would consult throughout the project.

Next came cleaning. Over a century, a varnish meant to protect the painting had yellowed and trapped soot. Okafor tested small mixtures of solvents on a corner no larger than a fingernail, looking for one that would dissolve the old varnish without disturbing the paint beneath. Only after weeks of testing did she begin cleaning the whole canvas, one small square at a time.

The cleaning revealed losses, small areas where paint had flaked away entirely. Here Okafor followed a rule shared by most conservators: anything she added must be reversible. She filled the gaps with a material that a future conservator could remove, and she painted only inside the missing areas, never over the artist's original work.

Finally, she applied a fresh varnish chosen to stay clear for decades. The landscape that emerged surprised everyone. The dark hills were in fact a bright autumn ridge, and a small boat with two figures, invisible for generations, appeared on the river.

The painting now hangs beside the photographs from Okafor's first step, so visitors can see both what was hidden and how patiently it was recovered.$q$, 'draft')
