-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f0a349e6-6e78-55ad-ac86-6916d81097b2', 'rg11-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Digit That Checks the Others$q$, $q$Every day, people type long strings of numbers into forms: library card numbers, shipping codes, account numbers. A single slip of the finger can send a package to the wrong address or charge the wrong account. Designers cannot stop people from making mistakes, but they have found a clever way to catch many of them before any harm is done. The trick is called a check digit.

A check digit is an extra number added to the end of a code. It is not chosen at random. Instead, it is calculated from all the other digits using a fixed rule. When someone types the code, the computer repeats the calculation on the digits that were entered and compares its answer with the final digit. If the two match, the code is accepted. If they do not, the computer knows something went wrong and asks the person to try again.

An example makes the idea concrete. Imagine a simple rule used by the fictional Brennick County Library: add up the digits of a card number and keep only the last digit of the total. For card 4725, the sum is 18, so the check digit is 8, and the full card number becomes 47258. If a patron mistypes it as 47358, the computer adds 4, 7, 3 and 5, gets 19, and expects a check digit of 9. Because the final digit is 8, the error is caught.

This simple rule has a weakness, however. If a patron swaps two neighboring digits and types 74258, the sum is unchanged, and the mistake slips through. Swapped digits are among the most common typing errors, so real systems use more elaborate rules, often multiplying every other digit by a different number before adding. That small change makes the order of the digits matter, and most swaps are then caught as well.

Check digits cannot fix a mistake, and no rule catches every possible error. But for the cost of one extra number, they stop a large share of everyday slips at the moment they happen, when they are easiest to correct.$q$, 'draft')
