   $q$Why is it effective that the author explains the general process in paragraph 2 before giving the library example?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It hides the weakness of the simple rule until readers trust it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It lets readers skip paragraph 2 entirely, since the example repeats it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-effect"},{"text":"Readers already know the steps, so the example can show each step in action rather than leaving them to guess the rule.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that the general process applies only to libraries.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Readers already know the steps, so the example can show each step in action rather than leaving them to guess the rule.$q$, $q$Knowing that the computer recalculates and compares, readers can follow the numbers in the example step by step. The example illustrates paragraph 2 rather than replacing it, and the process is presented as general, not library-only.$q$, 'draft', 'f0a349e6-6e78-55ad-ac86-6916d81097b2')
