  ('255ac435-c506-54db-9319-45d9d2d1a27b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The prefix ambi- means "both," and val comes from a root meaning "strength" or "worth." What does it mean that some coaches feel "ambivalent" about two-sided training?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They believe both hands should always get equal practice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"They are unaware that two-sided training is possible.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"They feel pulled between approval and concern at the same time.","is_correct":true},{"text":"They are completely opposed to training the weaker hand.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They feel pulled between approval and concern at the same time.$q$, $q$Ambivalent describes feelings of equal strength pulling both ways, which matches coaches who "admire the flexibility" yet "worry" about it. Complete opposition captures only the worry and ignores the admiration.$q$, 'draft', 'dc3eaa1d-85e2-5b3d-979e-c317babdffc8')
