-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('80f23e11-63de-5e85-9b63-0e0e4b2d0661', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Grow the Shoreline Back$q$, $q$Pellham Harbor faces a choice about how to protect its waterfront from storm waves. The town engineer has proposed a concrete seawall along two miles of shore at a cost of about 19 million dollars. The town should instead rebuild the oyster reefs that once lined the harbor, which would cost roughly 4 million dollars and protect the shore in ways a wall cannot.

Living reefs break waves before they reach land. In a three-year trial, researchers from the Saltmarsh Laboratory at Orrin College built a small reef of recycled shells off the southern beach. Instruments placed behind the reef recorded wave heights about 30 percent lower than those recorded at an unprotected stretch nearby.

A reef also changes with the sea, while a wall does not. As water levels slowly rise, oysters keep building on top of older shells, so a healthy reef grows upward over the decades. A concrete wall stays the height it was poured, and raising it later would add millions more to the bill.

Critics point out that a new reef needs several years to mature before it offers full protection. That is true, and the plan should be phased. Rows of heavy sandbags can guard the lowest streets while the reef grows, and the town can add reef sections each summer as funds allow.

The reef has already proven itself. During last October's storm, homes along Ferris Cove, a narrow inlet sheltered behind a rocky headland and the pilot reef, stayed dry, while several homes on the open eastern shore flooded.

A wall would draw a hard line between the town and the water. A reef would let the harbor heal itself while it protects the people who live beside it.$q$, 'draft')
