-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f4ad9a65-95ad-5369-877f-e13cfb2ac73c', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What Really Emptied the Tamsen Canal$q$, $q$For more than a century, local histories have told the same story about the Tamsen Canal. Opened in 1834 to carry grain and timber from the upper valley to the river port at Brissac, the canal flourished for two decades and then withered when the railroad arrived. The story is tidy, but the records suggest that the railroad delivered the final blow to a canal that was already failing.

The canal company's toll books survive in the county archive, and they tell a different tale. Boat traffic fell by roughly 40 percent between 1851 and 1853, a stretch of three dry summers in which rainfall reached barely half its usual level. The railroad did not reach the valley until 1858, five years after the steepest decline had already occurred.

The logs kept by boat captains explain why the drought mattered so much. Again and again, captains recorded that loaded boats ran aground in the shallow upper locks and had to be partly unloaded before they could pass. Shippers who lost days to these delays began sending their grain overland by wagon.

More surprising is what happened after the railroad opened. For the first four years of rail service, canal traffic held almost steady, because rail rates were high and many farmers still preferred the cheaper boats.

None of this means the railroad was harmless to the canal. By the late 1860s, lower rail rates had pulled away most of the remaining trade, and the canal closed in 1871. But a full account must begin with the drought. The railroad finished what the dry summers had started.$q$, 'draft')
