insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f703690a-a9cb-5303-a60e-c37844202b2b', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Trade the Textbooks for Tablets$q$, $q$Our district spends heavily on printed textbooks that are often outdated by the time they reach students. As a member of the Fairhollow school board, I urge my colleagues to replace printed textbooks in grades six through twelve with tablets loaded with digital editions, beginning next fall.

The results elsewhere are encouraging. Two years ago, the neighboring Linden Ridge district gave every middle school student a tablet. That same year, Linden Ridge also hired twelve new reading specialists for its middle schools. By the following spring, average reading scores had risen by 9 percent, which shows what digital learning can do.

The numbers also favor tablets. A full set of printed textbooks costs about 640 dollars per student and must be replaced every five years. A tablet with digital editions costs about 410 dollars over the same five years, a savings of more than 200 dollars per student. This estimate assumes that each tablet lasts the full five years without repair.

Tablets offer everyday benefits as well. Students would no longer carry backpacks weighing twenty pounds, and digital editions can be updated the moment a fact changes.

Some parents worry that more screen time will strain students' eyes. But students already stare at screens for hours every day, so a few more hours of reading on a tablet cannot make much difference.

Every year we wait, students learn from books that grow more out of date. The board should approve the switch this spring.$q$, 'draft')
