-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('55fa7369-65d4-5970-b16d-012a6bc69da2', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Volunteers Who Measure Rain$q$, $q$From an essay by climatologist Adaeze Morrow, written for the newsletter of a regional weather society.

Every morning at seven, a retired postal clerk named Teodor Halvorsen walks to the edge of his garden, lifts a plastic tube from its mount, and reads a number. Some mornings the number is zero. He writes it down anyway. He has done this for twenty-six years, and I would put his notebook up against any satellite I know.

That is not a sentimental claim. Satellites and radar estimate rainfall over wide areas, and they are remarkable tools, but an estimate is still an estimate. Radar sees the rain in the sky, not the rain that reaches the ground, and the two can differ by more than people expect. A gauge in a garden measures what actually fell. When thousands of volunteers like Teodor report from thousands of gardens, forecasters gain something no instrument in orbit can supply: a ground truth, checked daily, by people who live where the water lands.

Consider what his zeros are worth. A dry morning feels like nothing to record, and many volunteers are tempted to skip it. But a drought is made entirely of zeros. Without someone writing them down, a dry spell shows up in the record as a blank, and a blank cannot be told apart from a broken gauge or a volunteer on vacation. Teodor's patience is what turns silence into data.

I do not want to pretend this work is glamorous. It involves cold fingers in January, a gauge that occasionally fills with leaves, and the mild embarrassment of explaining to neighbors why you are staring into a tube. Nobody thanks the volunteers on the evening news when a flood warning goes out on time. Yet the warning is often checked against their reports.

So if you have ever wondered whether one person with a plastic tube can matter to the science of weather, I can answer plainly. One person cannot do much. Thousands of people, each faithful to a small, dull task, can tell us more about the rain than we could learn any other way.$q$, 'draft')
