   $q$How does the central comparison, "an error message is a letter," strengthen the author's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It suggests that error messages should be printed on paper and mailed to users after a problem occurs.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It recasts a technical string of text as human communication, implying the writer owes the reader clarity and courtesy.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It proves that error messages are longer than most other kinds of text found inside a software program.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-evidence-type"},{"text":"It shows that the author prefers writing letters by hand to designing software on a computer screen.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It recasts a technical string of text as human communication, implying the writer owes the reader clarity and courtesy.$q$, $q$By calling the message a letter written to a frustrated reader, she makes a design choice into a question of how we treat people. The comparison is figurative, not a proposal to mail anything.$q$, 'draft', 'b47fab89-20d0-5a29-9d95-b3fde7b1f6f0')
