  ('a61ff551-98c0-5de5-a66a-e54bc8ccec1e', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author writes, "Whoever wrote that message never meant to be rude. But the message was rude all the same." Why is this distinction effective for her audience?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It reveals that she believes most developers secretly enjoy confusing users and only pretend that their errors are accidents.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It excuses vague messages entirely, since the developers' good intentions matter more than the users' experience of them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-viewpoint"},{"text":"It shifts blame from developers to users, who should read error messages more carefully before calling a help line.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"It separates intention from effect, so she can criticize a common practice without attacking the developers she hopes to persuade.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It separates intention from effect, so she can criticize a common practice without attacking the developers she hopes to persuade.$q$, $q$Readers of a developer journal are the people who write these messages, so granting that they "never meant to be rude" keeps them from becoming defensive, while "rude all the same" keeps the criticism. She does not excuse the practice; the rest of the piece asks developers to change it.$q$, 'draft', 'b47fab89-20d0-5a29-9d95-b3fde7b1f6f0')
