-- Passage rg12-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f37a730c-34b2-54aa-a620-b762b8824e8f', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The First Portraits (1840s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Long Sitting (a story, 1844)

In the autumn of 1844, Hana Whitcombe's grandmother paid a full week's egg money for a portrait made by the new process everyone in Lowford was discussing. The traveling operator, Mr. Ignatius Bello, set up his apparatus in the back room of the inn, where a skylight let in the strongest afternoon light.

"You must not move at all," he told Hana, fitting an iron brace behind her head. "The plate remembers everything that holds still and nothing that does not."

Hana was known in Lowford for her laugh, which her friends claimed could be heard across the market. She tried to keep a small smile, but her cheeks soon ached, and by the end of the sitting her face had settled into a stern calm she did not recognize.

When Mr. Bello tilted the finished plate so the silver image appeared, Hana was disappointed. Every thread of her collar was there, and the tiny scar on her chin, yet the girl in the picture looked as if she had never laughed in her life.

"It is not me," she said.

Her grandmother held the plate near the window for a long time. "It is not how you are," she agreed at last. "It is proof that you were here. Someday that will matter more than the laugh."

Hana did not believe her that afternoon. Still, she wrapped the plate in flannel and carried it home with both hands.

Text 2: The Painter to the Camera (a poem, 1844)

You catch the button and the frayed lace cuff,
The crooked tooth, the dust upon the sill.
You never tire, and never look away,
And all you ask of anyone is: hold still.

But I have spent a lifetime learning how
To choose the single moment worth the keeping:
The lifted brow before a joke is told,
The softened mouth of someone almost sleeping.

You give us every detail, and no choice.
I make one choice and let the rest go free.
So tell me, honest box of glass and light,
Is all you keep the person that I see?$q$, 'draft')
