   $q$How does the grandmother's response in Text 1 complicate the question that ends Text 2?$q$, $q$[{"text":"She answers the painter directly by proving that the photograph shows Hana's laugh more truly than any painting.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"},{"text":"She repeats the painter's worry word for word, showing that the two texts reach exactly the same conclusion.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstatement"},{"text":"She grants that the image misses Hana's nature but finds a different value in it, as a record that Hana existed, which the painter does not consider.","is_correct":true},{"text":"She agrees with the painter that the camera is worthless and that only a painted portrait deserves to be kept.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$She grants that the image misses Hana's nature but finds a different value in it, as a record that Hana existed, which the painter does not consider.$q$, $q$The grandmother admits "It is not how you are" yet calls the plate "proof that you were here," a kind of worth the poem's question about capturing the person never weighs. She never calls the camera worthless; Hana even carries the plate home carefully.$q$, 'draft', 'f37a730c-34b2-54aa-a620-b762b8824e8f')
