   $q$How do the sources of weather knowledge in the two texts differ?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both rely mainly on letters from the weather office to tell them when storms will arrive.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Lucinda's numbers gain meaning when combined with distant reports, while Amos's knowledge comes from his own senses on one hillside.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lucinda's knowledge is passed down from her father, while Amos learned everything from books.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Lucinda learns by smelling the air and watching birds, while Amos relies on a ledger and a brass gauge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-texts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Lucinda's numbers gain meaning when combined with distant reports, while Amos's knowledge comes from his own senses on one hillside.$q$, $q$Lucinda's zeros matter once the office joins them with reports from "more than two hundred stations," while Amos reads swallows, smoke and the smell of clover. Swapping the methods confuses the two texts.$q$, 'draft', 'f97f9e7c-d645-5461-8e9a-6c7bef3b8b24')
