-- Passage rg12-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0ffe9028-d76f-5540-8d17-2fe7e203e06b', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The City Marathon (1980s)$q$, $q$Text 1: Mile Twenty-Two (a story, 1983)

By mile twenty-two, Femi Castellano had stopped looking at his watch. The leaders had finished more than an hour earlier, and the volunteers at the water tables were stacking empty cups into towers. A street sweeper idled behind the last cluster of runners like a patient dog.

He had trained for eight months, mostly in the dark before his shifts at the bakery, and he had imagined this stretch differently. In his imagination, the crowd still lined the curb. In fact, only a few people remained: a boy on a bicycle, a woman with a folding chair, a man who clapped for each runner with the steady rhythm of someone keeping time.

Femi's calves were burning. A van marked for runners who wished to quit rolled slowly beside him, its side door open. He looked at it for a long moment.

Then he thought about the eight months of dark mornings, and how the city had looked at five o'clock, empty and blue, as though it belonged to him alone. He had not trained to be fast. He had trained to find out whether he could keep a promise to himself.

The van pulled ahead. Femi kept running.

Text 2: For the Last Runner (a poem, 1983)

The banners sag. The fastest ones are home.
The stopwatch on the scaffold has been stilled.
The crowd that roared at noon has drifted off
To lunches and to errands unfulfilled.

But I have kept my place beside the tape,
A volunteer with nothing left to hand,
Because the race is not the race until
The last of them comes stumbling where I stand.

There is no prize for this. There is no time
That anyone will copy into print.
There is a person, and a finish line,
And the road behind them, and the will in it.

So let me be the cheer they did not hear
When morning's thousands lined the avenue.
The winner ran for records. You, my friend,
Ran for the plain and stubborn sake of through.$q$, 'draft')
