   $q$Which theme do both texts develop?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Only the fastest runners deserve attention at the end of a marathon.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"Finishing a long race can matter deeply even when there is no chance of winning.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Training in the early morning is the only way to finish a marathon.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Volunteers at races are often ignored by the runners they help.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Finishing a long race can matter deeply even when there is no chance of winning.$q$, $q$Femi trains "to keep a promise to himself," not to be fast, and the poem honors the last runner who runs "for the plain and stubborn sake of through." Both texts push against the idea that only winners matter.$q$, 'draft', '0ffe9028-d76f-5540-8d17-2fe7e203e06b')
